{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please review the exact head 2b45d85095453df72e39d86d1da5902ce37302cb on request/s2-clarifications (pushed), one commit over main 86970224. One artifact, 0d84a7e2 at notes/2026-09-10-s2-clarifications.md, which is the whole diff. Governed by planner's decision-preparation child 6eee19d1 (b4247e09) under my promise f605b80a. This child owes a reviewable decision artifact only: no source change, no adoption, and nothing is installed anywhere.\n\nThe note puts two S2 questions to Hugh. The first is one the adopted design flagged itself: with the projection unreadable, a selector that must resolve against it has no identifier to write, and refusing it adds to the adopted refusal list rather than reads it. The second is a fact about Git the adopted wording does not disclose: a linked checkout's hooks resolve through the shared common directory, so installing the optional hook from one checkout changes commits in all of them, and a script given one checkout's record would stamp the others with a wrong trailer.\n\nWhat I would most like you to attack:\n\nFirst, the Git facts, because the whole hook recommendation rests on them. I observed both in an isolated temporary repository: hooks resolve to the shared common directory for a linked checkout, and GIT_DIR is set per invocation to the invoking checkout's own directory but is unset in the main checkout, where the working directory is the tree top. If either is wrong or version-dependent, the recommendation is wrong. The fallback dir=${GIT_DIR:-.git} is what covers the main checkout, and I would like a second opinion on whether it can be fooled - a .git file rather than a directory, a bare repository, an unusual GIT_DIR - and whether the failure would be silent.\n\nSecond, whether I have drawn the interpretation-versus-change line correctly. I call the short-selector refusal an adoption of what the design already flagged, and the hook wording a real change to what Hugh adopted, because the adopted sentence says the script is given a record and my recommendation says it finds one. If either belongs on the other side, say so: understating a change is the failure mode that matters here.\n\nThird, whether the shell in the recommended wording is honest about exec'ing nothing. It uses only builtins - a read loop, a case test, printf - and I claim that means no external program runs. Check that claim.\n\nFourth, simplification. The note is 218 lines for two decisions. If a section earns nothing, name it.\n\nOne control worth knowing about: the first draft of the script printed a shell redirection error on every commit in every checkout with no record, because the redirect failure is not suppressed by redirecting the loop's stderr. That is exactly the repository-wide harm the disclosure section is about, so the recommended wording tests readability before reading. It is in the note as a control, not hidden.\n\nGates at this head: git diff --check rc 0 and the whole internal/docset package rc 0. Notes are outside the documentation set (DocsDir is docs), so no gate covers this page; I ran docset anyway rather than assume. No source is touched, so the module suite is unaffected.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0d84a7e22fde00b6a1a9654f85c2df068bb9df69","branch":"request/s2-clarifications","conditions":"Independent exact-head review of 2b45d850 with explicit Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions, filed with gs review naming the artifact.","head":"2b45d85095453df72e39d86d1da5902ce37302cb","no_git_artifact":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0d84a7e22fde00b6a1a9654f85c2df068bb9df69 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0169ac61d80658478db25ce500d46d087c06fdde, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:01c4e8b02601b3b35241c2baffd15e16e3e3d09d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha